# Install a 2-node Kubernetes cluster with kubeadm

Target version: **Kubernetes v1.37** (released 26 Aug 2026, supported until 28 Oct 2027).
Verified against the upstream kubeadm documentation for v1.37.

> If you only want to run the examples in this repo and do not need a
> multi-node cluster, skip all of this and use `current_practice/00-kind-cluster.yaml`.
> One command, no VMs, about three minutes.

## Pre-requisites

    OS       : Ubuntu 22.04 LTS or 24.04 LTS
    CPU      : 2 cores or more (control plane needs 2)
    Memory   : 2 GB or more per machine, 4 GB recommended
    Disk     : 30 GB or more
    Network  : bridged, full connectivity between the nodes
    Access   : passwordless sudo for the installing user

Every node needs a unique hostname, MAC address and product_uuid.

## Install the control plane

1. Log in to the control-plane node, clone this repo:

       git clone https://github.com/cloud-dev-user/kubernetes_demo.git
       cd kubernetes_demo/installation_scripts

2. Put the IPv4 addresses of both nodes into `hostname.txt` (a sample is in
   this repo), then run:

       ./pre_configure_k8s_master.sh

   To install a different minor version, set `KUBE_VERSION` first — the
   package repository URL contains the minor version, so this is not
   optional:

       KUBE_VERSION=v1.36 ./pre_configure_k8s_master.sh

3. Initialise the cluster. The pod CIDR must match what your CNI expects;
   `192.168.0.0/16` is Calico's default:

       sudo kubeadm init \
         --control-plane-endpoint=k8smaster.ssktraining.com \
         --pod-network-cidr=192.168.0.0/16

   Wait for it to finish and copy the whole output somewhere safe.

4. Set up kubectl for your user:

       mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
       sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
       sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config

## Add the worker

5. Log in to the worker node, clone this repo and run the worker script:

       git clone https://github.com/cloud-dev-user/kubernetes_demo.git
       cd kubernetes_demo/installation_scripts
       ./pre_configure_k8s_worker.sh

   Update `hostname.txt` first, same as on the control plane.

6. Join it to the cluster with the command `kubeadm init` printed in step 3:

       sudo kubeadm join k8smaster.ssktraining.com:6443 \
         --token <token> \
         --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:<hash>

   Lost that line? On the control plane:

       kubeadm token create --print-join-command

7. Back on the control plane:

       kubectl get nodes

   Both nodes appear, both `NotReady`. That is correct — there is no pod
   network yet.

       NAME                        STATUS     ROLES           AGE   VERSION
       k8smaster.ssktraining.com   NotReady   control-plane   9m    v1.37.1
       k8sworker.ssktraining.com   NotReady   <none>          1m    v1.37.1

## Install the pod network

8. Calico, via its operator (the method Tigera recommends for new clusters):

       kubectl create -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/v3.32.2/manifests/tigera-operator.yaml
       kubectl create -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/v3.32.2/manifests/custom-resources.yaml

   The older single-manifest install still works if you prefer it:

       kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/v3.32.2/manifests/calico.yaml

9. Verify:

       kubectl get pods -A
       kubectl get nodes

   Both nodes go `Ready` once Calico is up.

Your cluster is ready.

## Verify it does what you think it does

Two checks worth running before you trust it:

    # NetworkPolicy is actually enforced (many CNIs accept the object and ignore it)
    kubectl apply -f current_practice/13-network-policy.yaml

    # Pod Security Admission is on
    kubectl apply -f current_practice/01-namespace-pod-security.yaml

The second one is meant to fail — see `current_practice/README.md`.

